summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Womack is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. The patient is doing okay. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. This is mostly in her elbows. She has been sitting at the computer, at the desk more, in the office so she does not know if this is being stagnant in this position all day has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. She is right-handed and her right elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing so it- it flares up in both. She denies any other joint pain. She does feel some numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She denies any numbness or tingling in her feet. She has had a history of gout. The last episode she had was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She reports that the medication I gave her really controlled it and she has not seen a flare up since. She also reports that her psoriasis is under control. She has not had any major problems with that. She is using the clobetasol for her scalp and she says that is doing a lot better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain, left elbow pain, and stiffness. Denies lower extremity pain.
Skin: Reports psoriasis.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Hematologic: Denies joint pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination of the Right Elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Tenderness and effusion right there. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Stiffness of the right elbow.

RESULTS

Autoimmune labs are within normal limits.

X-rays of the right elbow ordered and will be performed.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems.
Gout.
- Medical Reasoning: Her last episode was 3 months ago.
- Medical Treatment: We will continue her on Colcrys 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of her gout.
Osteoarthritis.
- Medical Reasoning: She has a history of this and her x-ray will show the extent of the damage.
- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and swelling.
Psoriasis.
- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well on the clobetasol for her scalp.
- Medical Treatment: We will continue her on the clobetasol.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
INSTRUCTIONS

We will review the x-ray of her right elbow and then refer her for physical therapy as needed. }}<|eot_id|>
