Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Ms. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for back pain. The onset of her pain began when she fell down the stairs at a birthday party. She states that she was at a surprise party and they were all huddled behind a couch, and when the person came in they all jumped up and she fell down the stairs and rammed her back into the railing of the staircase. She reports that it was a twisting motion that caused her pain. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She notes that it hurts more when she is lying flat and any twisting motion makes it worse. She has a history of lumbar fusion.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Normal strength in the lower extremities. Sensation is intact to light touch distally. No numbness or tingling.

RESULTS

X-ray of the lumbar spine was reviewed and it shows a lumbar fusion at T4. Otherwise, the exam is normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 40-year-old female who presents today with back pain. She has a history of lumbar fusion. She fell down the stairs at a birthday party and sustained a twisting motion to her back. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. Her symptoms are consistent with a strain. I have prescribed the patient Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. I also advised her to use ice and heat alternating on her back. I want her to ambulate. If her symptoms do not improve or worsen, I want her to come back and see me in the next week.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>
