summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Ms. Rebecca Brown is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of her pain started 1 month ago after she began swimming more regularly. She denies any injury or trauma to her knee. She notes that she is only having pain in her right knee while swimming. She is using the breaststroke technique with her legs kicking out. She is able to swim 10 laps initially, but after a couple of laps, she starts to feel pain in her right knee. She denies any pain down her leg.

Ms. Brown has a past medical history of carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release, chronic back pain, and depression. She has been taking medication for her depression and has been seeing a therapist. She has been doing well with this. She has been getting outside more and has been doing well with her back pain. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.

Ms. Brown denies any knee pain outside of swimming. She denies any back pain. She denies any pain down her legs. She denies any joint swelling.

MEDICATIONS

She is currently taking medication for depression.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.
EENT: Denies dysphagia.
Psychiatric: Denies suicidal or homicidal ideations.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint swelling.
Skin: Denies rashes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: No swelling. Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with range of motion of the knee.
MSK: Examination of the back: No midline tenderness. No pain with straight leg raising.
Neuro: Normal strength in the lower extremities. No numbness or tingling.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee is reviewed and is normal.

ASSESSMENT

Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing right knee pain after swimming. She has been swimming more regularly for the past month. She has a history of carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release. I believe her symptoms are due to chondromalacia of the patella.

Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on medication and seeing a therapist. She is doing well with this. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations. I believe her depression is well-controlled at this time.

Carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with this.

PLAN

Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Treatment: I am prescribing ibuprofen 600 mg four times a day with food. I would like her to avoid activities that put extra pressure on her knees. I would like her to do straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps. I would like her to avoid the breaststroke while swimming. If she is comfortable, she can try other swimming strokes where her knees are straight. I would like her to avoid lunges. I would like her to follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve.

Depression.
• Medical Treatment: I am going to keep her on her current medication regimen.

Carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release.
• Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.

INSTRUCTIONS

Please follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>
