summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

High blood sugar.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Hannah Smith is a 52-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of new onset high blood sugar. The patient reports that she was in the emergency room recently and was told that her blood sugar was high. The patient states that she has been eating a lot of sweets lately. The patient explains that there are always sweet things available at work and at her family gatherings during the holidays. The patient notes that she felt fine at first but started to feel lightheaded and saw spots when she started to feel dizzy. The patient was given intravenous fluids and told to cut out extra sweets and return to her normal diet. The patient is concerned about returning to her normal diet as she is still at her family's house and her mother and grandmother are big bakers. The patient denies any fevers, chills, chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, burning with urination, joint pain or muscle aches. The patient has a history of atrial fibrillation and osteoarthritis. The patient notes that she has been doing well with her gastroesophageal reflux and that her medication has been helping.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: The patient appears in no distress.

Neck: Thyroid is mildly enlarged and non-tender.

Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm.

Respiratory: Lungs are clear.

Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft and nontender.

Musculoskeletal: No edema in the lower extremities.

RESULTS

Blood glucose is elevated. Hemoglobin A1c is also elevated.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Atrial fibrillation.
Medical Reasoning: She is currently in atrial fibrillation, but she is in a good rate. We will continue her on metoprolol 25 mg twice a day. She does need a refill on that.

Gastroesophageal reflux.
Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well with her GERD. She is currently taking protonix 40 mg once a day.

Diabetes.
Medical Reasoning: She has recently been diagnosed with diabetes. We are not sure if it is type 1 or type 2. We will order some labs to work that up. We will start her on metformin 500 mg twice a day. If her labs show autoimmune disorder, we will start her on insulin.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in a month. She will continue her metoprolol 25 mg twice a day for her atrial fibrillation. She will continue her protonix 40 mg once a day for her gastroesophageal reflux. She will start metformin 500 mg twice a day for her diabetes. She will also need a refill on her metoprolol. }}<|eot_id|>
