summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Emily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents today for a yearly follow-up of her asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy. Due to insurance changes since her last office appointment in April 2020, she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela. She also uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms, which tend to work well. She also has known peanut allergy, she does tolerate almonds and cashews.

The patient last saw me in April 2020 for a telemedicine visit. At that time, she was on Symbicort. She has been doing well since that time.

The patient is currently on Wixela 200/50, one puff twice a day. She reports that it works well for her. She also uses her albuterol inhaler rarely, only before a workout or strenuous activity. She is currently trying to reduce her daily use of Wixela since she is trying to get into the military soon. She has been trying to use her daily inhaler less and less. She has been doing well on this regimen. She denies any urgent care or ER visits for breathing over the past year.

Regarding her allergies, she typically uses her Zyrtec in the spring and fall. She is currently in the midst of spring, and she has been taking it on days when she knows she will be outside a lot. She works outside 3 days a week, so she takes it on those days. She reports that it works well for her.

Regarding her peanut allergy, she is still avoiding peanuts. She has had some testing done in the past for tree nuts, but she does tolerate almonds and cashews. She has been careful about avoiding all tree nuts. She has not had any reactions since we last saw her. She has an EpiPen that she keeps with her.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or changes in appetite.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing or coughing.
Skin: Denies rashes or hives.
Allergic: Denies anaphylaxis.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Ears: Bilateral cerumen. Neck: No lymphadenopathy.
Respiratory: Lungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales or rhonchi.

RESULTS

Breathing test results are reviewed and are comparable to her prior results from 2019. Her numbers have actually improved since that time.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Asthma.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well on her current regimen and her breathing test results are comparable to her prior results from 2019.
• Medical Treatment: Continue using Wixela 200/50 one puff twice a day. I will call in a new Alvesco inhaler, 80 mcg, two puffs twice a day for her to try. We will repeat her breathing test in 4 months to document improvement.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to use the Alvesco only when she is ill or during smoke season. She should also use her albuterol inhaler as needed.

Allergic Rhinitis.
• Medical Reasoning: Her symptoms are well controlled on Zyrtec.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to use over the counter Zyrtec 10 mg as needed.

Peanut Allergy.
• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well and avoiding peanuts.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. She has an EpiPen that she keeps with her.

Allergies.
• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well on her current regimen.
• Medical Treatment: Continue to use her albuterol inhaler as needed. Continue to use her Zyrtec as needed.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue to use the Wixela 200/50 one puff twice a day. Continue to use the albuterol inhaler as needed. Continue to use the Zyrtec as needed. Continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. Follow up in 4 months for a breathing test and spirometry. }}<|eot_id|>
