summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Palpitations and hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension and palpitations. The patient was seen in the emergency room for palpitations and high blood pressure. The patient states that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He has a new primary care doctor who started him on a new medication 1 month ago. He is currently taking Benicar HCT 40-25, which he calls his “water pill”, and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and has been running 146-155 mmHg. He is trying to reduce his salt intake and is doing better. He has been walking on the treadmill when he can. He denies any recent episodes of high blood pressure.

MEDICATIONS

Benicar HCT 40-25 mg daily.
Amlodipine 10 mg daily.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional
- General Examination: In no apparent distress.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops or rubs, and no S3 or S4.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

RESULTS

Thyroid profile from 03/01/2021 is unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his hypertension and palpitations. The patient was seen in the emergency room for palpitations and high blood pressure. The patient states that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. He has a new primary care doctor who started him on a new medication 1 month ago. He is currently taking Benicar HCT 40-25, which he calls his “water pill”, and Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and has been running 146-155 mmHg. He is trying to reduce his salt intake and is doing better. He has been walking on the treadmill when he can. He denies any recent episodes of high blood pressure.

The patient was started on spironolactone today. I discussed the possible side effects of this medication including dizziness, tenderness in the chest, swollen breasts, and electrolyte imbalance. I also discussed the importance of reducing his salt intake and staying hydrated. I encouraged him to continue with his treadmill walking. I advised him to follow up in 2 weeks for a blood pressure check and electrolyte panel. If he does not respond to the spironolactone, we will consider the resistant hypertension clinic.

PREFERRED PHARMACIES

CVI Medical Supply.
CVI Medical Supply.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
 }}<|eot_id|>
