summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

The patient reports he has not been sick or hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He states he has improved since his surgery. The patient states he is able to walk to the dining room now. He denies any headaches, pain where the shunt is, or pain in his belly. The patient reports he has improved cognition and is able to have a normal conversation with his daughter, Hannah.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
History of progressive gait impairment.
History of cognitive impairment.
History of decreased bladder control.
History of urinary incontinence.
History of urinary urgency.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient denies smoking, alcohol, or drug use.

FAMILY HISTORY

Family history is significant for gait impairment.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

The patient's medications are reviewed and unchanged.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional
Impaired ability to carry out daily functions.
No fever or unintentional weight loss.

GU
Urinary incontinence and urgency. No genital sores, decreased or painful output.

Neurological
Walking impairment.
Cognitive impairment.
No headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.

PSYCHIATRIC
Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate 64, respirations 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain 0/10.

RESULTS

The patient was tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration in fund of knowledge. He scored a 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. At the time, he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice.

Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.

Neurological
Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarchria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no- finger to nose.

RESULTS

CT scan from the head of 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading there is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus.
Gait impairment.
Urinary incontinence and urgency.
Cognitive impairment.

The patient had a shunt placed on 08/01/2020. He has improved since his surgery. I would like to leave the shunt set for another 2-3 months before we go in changing it. Like I said, if he is not having any headaches or pain and he has improved so that is all good for me. So really there is nothing to worry about.

The patient will follow up in 3 months. He is unlikely to drive at this time, but we can continue to monitor his improvement and discuss driving at that time. He could also enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
