summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history is significant for significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04-16-2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports that she has been drinking for 4 to 5 days a week, hard alcohol. She is taking her medications as prescribed. She reports that the first time she took the lisinopril she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She reports that she feels much better and that it is definitely helping but that she does not know that it will be okay until she stops drinking. She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is doing home blood pressure checks. She is taking the lisinopril and the hydrochlorothiazide in the morning. She reports that her blood pressure is lower in the morning than in the evening. She has been taking her medications at the same time each day. She has tried medications in the past and she thinks that this may be the one that works.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Significant alcohol use disorder.
Mild hypercholesterolemia.

SOCIAL HISTORY

She reports that she drinks 4 to 5 days a week.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily.
Hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily.

RESULTS

None to report.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional
Alert, pleasant, in no acute distress.

Cardiovascular
No CVA tenderness. Regular rate and rhythm. No murmur, gallop, or rub.

Respiratory
Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Unlabored breathing.

Skin
Moist.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up for hypertension. Her history is significant for significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04-16-2021 her blood pressure was 130/90. After presenting at 150/100 she was prescribed hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg by mouth once daily and lisinopril 20 mg by mouth once daily. CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

The patient reports that she has been drinking for 4 to 5 days a week, hard alcohol. She is taking her medications as prescribed. She reports that the first time she took the lisinopril she felt lethargic and tired, so she only took a half dose the next time. She reports that she feels much better and that it is definitely helping but that she does not know that it will be okay until she stops drinking. She is taking her blood pressure medications. She is taking the full dose of the hydrochlorothiazide. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is doing home blood pressure checks. She is taking the lisinopril and the hydrochlorothiazide in the morning. She reports that her blood pressure is lower in the morning than in the evening. She has been taking her medications at the same time each day. She has tried medications in the past and she thinks that this may be the one that works.

A follow-up with the alcohol use disorder specialist is recommended.

The patient would like to start Naltrexone which we have previously discussed. She will take the injectable form and start on 05-17-2021. We reviewed side effects and risks. The potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to her for many reasons. Hypertension, she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. Quite possibly her blood pressure will improve completely with cessation of alcohol. Her target BP is 130 to 140/70 to 80. I will see her back week of 06-14-2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection.

I recommend upgrading influenza vaccination and Shingrix. #2, she did defer this.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue her current medicines. Keep checking her blood pressure. Let me know if she has any questions or any issues come up after she receives the first dose.
 }}<|eot_id|>
