summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee swelling and pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow up of his right knee swelling and pain. The patient reports that his pain level is currently 3 out of 10, but typically it is around 6. The patient notes that his right knee has been swollen for the past 2 months. He states that the swelling has worsened over the past few weeks. He is unable to ambulate without the use of a cane. The patient states that the pain is worse when he first gets up from sitting. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily for the past 2 months.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Rheumatoid arthritis.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he loves salty snacks.

MEDICATIONS

Xeljanz 11 mg daily
Celebrex 200 mg daily

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Normal capillary refill in the toes. The right knee is warm to the touch. There is pitting edema around the right knee and ankle.
MSK: Examination of the right knee: Swelling, erythema, and tenderness to palpation. Range of motion is slightly diminished. Effusion was attempted, but there was no fluid.

ASSESSMENT

• Rheumatoid arthritis.
• Right knee swelling.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and medical history today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I explained that the right knee is swollen due to inflammation. I have recommended that we give him another steroid injection in the right knee. I have also recommended that we get an x-ray of the right knee to see where the swelling is coming from. The patient would like to have the injection today. I have also recommended that we set up physical therapy for him. I have explained that the swelling could be due to salt retention, so I advised him to reduce his salt intake. The patient states that he has already been wearing compression stockings. I advised him to continue to wear them and to elevate his legs at night. I have also recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician if his symptoms do not improve. I have ordered an x-ray of the right knee. The risks, benefits, and alternatives of the x-ray were explained. The risks, benefits, and alternatives of the steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.

PROCEDURE

Right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>
