summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash on the face.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a pleasant 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. She reports that her rash is doing much better. She notes that she has some leftover rash, but this is expected. She has been using doxycycline 100 mg once a day for the past week and she started to notice some improvement. She was taking it twice a day initially, but then she started to notice a plateau and decided to limit it to once a day. She is not breastfeeding at this time. She denies using any other medications.

The patient was started on doxycycline 100 mg twice a day 2 weeks ago. She was also prescribed Elidel 1% cream, but she did not want to use it. She was also prescribed MetroGel 1% cream, which she has been using after washing her face with Bruders face wash. She has also been using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day.

The patient reports that she is sensitive to the sun.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Rosacea.
Perioral dermatitis.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Breastfeeding: No.

MEDICATIONS

• Doxycycline 100 mg twice a day.
• Elidel 1% cream.
• MetroGel 1% cream.
• Bruders face wash.
• Sulfacetamide wash.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Bilateral medial cheeks: There are a few faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Perioral dermatitis.
Rosacea.
Ocular rosacea.

Patient has been using doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for the past 2 weeks. We will continue this for another week and then she will stop. She will also continue using MetroGel 1% cream after washing her face with Bruders face wash. We will start Elidel 1% cream today. She will apply it to the rash on her face twice a day until the rash is gone and then once a day for another week before stopping. She should hold off until she sees me again before using her old products. If it starts to flair up again, she can try using Elidel for a couple of days, to see if that is enough. If it is not, then she can restart the doxycycline as well. I do not expect this to happen, but if it does, she should call me or send me a MyChart message. She should also continue to use a moisturizer. I will give her a couple of samples of Vanicream.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the benefits and risks of the recommended treatment plan. I explained that the doxycycline is a low risk medication and that it should clear up quickly. The patient was concerned about using Elidel, but I explained that it is not a steroid and that it is not going to cause any other kind of rash. I also explained that it is not going to cause skin thinning. We discussed that she should use it until the rash is gone and then once a day for another week before stopping. I also explained that if she starts to notice a flare up, she can try using Elidel for a couple of days, to see if that is enough. If it is not, then she can restart the doxycycline as well. I explained that this should not happen, but if it does, she should call me or send me a MyChart message. The patient was advised to stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing, any kind of plant thing, and to really let's just be sure we talk about any new products, or reach out to me if she is going to try something else.

Patient Handoffs

The patient will be referred to Ophthalmology for her ocular rosacea. }}<|eot_id|>
