summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Miller is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of his abdominal pain. The patient was seen by Dr. Miller in the office 3 weeks ago for the same complaint. The patient reports that he has been having pain on the right side of his abdomen, specifically underneath his ribs. He describes it as a squeezing pain that comes and goes. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 in severity. He states that it is associated with nausea. He denies any fevers or vomiting. The patient reports that he has a history of type 2 diabetes and was initially going to be managed with diet and exercise, but he has since started a keto diet which has resulted in a 25-pound weight loss over the past 3 months. The patient has a family history of gallbladder disease, both his mother and father required cholecystectomy.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers.
Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain. Denies nausea and vomiting.
Genitourinary: Denies hematuria.
Musculoskeletal: Denies back pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs.

Gastrointestinal
- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild wall thickening is appreciated. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiologic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I explained to him that he has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I explained that he has not had any improvement in his symptoms despite dietary modifications. I have recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy to remove his gallbladder. He will undergo the procedure in the next week. He will spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He will need to avoid high fat foods after the surgery. I also discussed his type 2 diabetes with him today. I advised him to follow up with his primary care physician to ensure he is managing his diabetes well.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week after his surgery. }}<|eot_id|>
