summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right shoulder pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Matthew Smith is a 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right shoulder pain. The onset of his pain began 4 days ago after he was tackled during a game of football. The patient states he was playing football and was landed on his shoulder in an unusual manner. He denies numbness or tingling in his fingers, but reports pain when he attempts to lift his arm overhead. He has been taking Tylenol, but it has not provided much relief. He denies any pain in his neck, but does report a slight headache. He denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his right arm. He denies any prior orthopedic injuries to his right shoulder. He does have a history of appendicitis and had an appendectomy 3 to 4 years ago.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain.
Neurological: Denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in his right arm.
Skin: Denies any bruising or ecchymosis.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 122/76, heart rate is 82, and oxygenation is 95% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

