HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a pleasant 57-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up after her recent interior ST elevation myocardial infarction. The patient had an RCA stent placed in 2018. The patient reports that she was in her normal state of health until she started having chest pain and was taken to the emergency room. She was diagnosed with another stent placement. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily for a mile and a half to two miles. She denies any leg swelling. She is able to lie flat in bed at night, but uses two pillows. She reports that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She reports that she has not been watching her salt intake. She reports that pizza is her favorite food. She reports that she has been watching her sugars and has been walking her dog. She denies any shortness of breath.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that she is compliant with her medications. She takes aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fatigue.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Musculoskeletal: Reports lower extremity swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
- General Examination: No jugular venous distension.
- Carotid: No bruits.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination: Lower extremities show 1+ pitting edema.

RESULTS

EKG reveals normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes are anticipated given the patient's history of recent STEMI.

Echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. There is moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 57-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after her recent interior ST elevation myocardial infarction. She has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.
1. Coronary artery disease.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. We will continue to monitor her ejection fraction and her mitral regurgitation.
- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue cardiac rehab and to exercise regularly. I also encouraged her to work on her diet and to limit her salt intake.
- Additional Testing: We will repeat an echocardiogram in 2 months.
2. Hypertension.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and she is currently tolerating her blood pressure well.
- Medical Treatment: We will continue her Lisinopril 20 mg daily. She will also start Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to work with a nutritionist to consider other diet options.
3. Reduced left ventricular dysfunction.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. I believe that her heart function will recover given her compliance with her medications.
- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take Lisinopril 20 mg daily.
4. Moderate mitral regurgitation.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. I believe that her mitral regurgitation will improve over time.
- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take Lisinopril 20 mg daily.
5. Diabetes.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been compliant with her medications and her diet.
- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take metformin.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
