summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right hand numbness and tingling.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Alexis Smith is a pleasant 40-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right hand numbness and tingling. She states that she sits at a keyboard all day long and has been experiencing this for about 6 months, but it has been more persistent over the last month. She reports intermittent pain in her right hand when typing on the keyboard and using a mouse. She has been taking ibuprofen for the pain. She denies any pain at night.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a history of a right rotator cuff repair.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient works at a computer all day.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports taking ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right hand numbness and tingling.
Neurological: Reports right hand numbness and tingling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
- Examination of the right hand: Grip strength is 4/5. Tinel's sign positive over the right wrist.

ASSESSMENT

Right hand numbness and tingling.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and history, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have recommended a wrist splint for her to wear at night. I have also recommended that she take ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day. I have ordered an electromyography/nerve conduction study for further evaluation. I have instructed her to continue with physical therapy if the nerve conduction study is positive. If the nerve conduction study is positive, we will discuss the option of surgery.

INSTRUCTIONS

Follow-up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>
