summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Philip Johnson is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for an evaluation of right elbow pain. The onset of pain began 1 week ago after he was hit by his son with a lacrosse stick. He denies any recent trauma. He describes pain at the tip of the elbow, which radiates down the forearm. He has tried taking Advil, but he usually forgets about it. He denies any swelling, redness, or deformity. He has been playing lacrosse with his kids, and he believes he is getting too old for it. He is an active person who enjoys playing with his kids.

He has a past medical history of hypertension, which is controlled on 20 mg of lisinopril. He has been compliant with his medication. He also has diabetes, which is controlled on 500 mg of metformin. He does not take insulin. He denies any recent weight changes, but he admits he fell off his diet during the pandemic. He has been compliant with his diet, and he follows a Mediterranean diet. He denies any recent alcohol or tobacco use. He denies any recent chest pain, shortness of breath, or abdominal pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional
- General Examination: In no acute distress.
- Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
- Cardiovascular: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. No S3 or S4 gallop. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination of the right elbow: Pain to palpation over the olecranon area of the posterior elbow. Mild pain with flexion and extension of the right elbow. Normal range of motion. X-ray of the right elbow was performed today and was normal.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right elbow was reviewed and is normal.

ASSESSMENT

1. Right elbow contusion.
2. Hypertension.
3. Diabetes mellitus.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his right elbow pain is consistent with an elbow contusion. I have recommended that he take 800 mg of ibuprofen twice daily to help with pain and inflammation. I have also recommended icing his right elbow 3 times per day for 20 minutes at a time. I have advised him to avoid playing lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks to allow his elbow to heal.
Regarding his hypertension, I have recommended that we continue him on 20 mg of lisinopril daily. He has been compliant with his medication and diet. We will order a hemoglobin A1c to check his blood sugar levels.
Regarding his diabetes, we will continue him on 500 mg of metformin daily. He denies any recent weight changes, but he admits he fell off his diet during the pandemic. He has been compliant with his diet, and he follows a Mediterranean diet. He denies any recent alcohol or tobacco use.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>
