summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Acute on chronic heart failure.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Johnson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic heart failure. He reports that he was doing well until he noticed that his ankles were swelling and he became short of breath. He called 911 and the paramedics placed him on a special mask. He was then evaluated at the Emergency Department where his blood pressure was noted to be 200/90 mmHg. He states that he has been trying to watch his diet as instructed. He has been taking his medications, but sometimes misses a dose or two. He is a big cheese lover and enjoys watching football and going to parties on the weekends. He loves pizza and chicken wings. He reports that he has been feeling well since his hospitalization. He has been urinating a lot and has been on a water pill through his IV. He has been taking his medications for his blood pressure as instructed. He has been checking his blood pressure at home and it has been good. He has been watching his diet and has not had any shortness of breath or chest pain. He has been sleeping well and has been taking an afternoon nap. He denies any chest pain. He reports that he has had a blood pressure cuff at home and has been checking his blood pressure every other day.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports taking Bumex 2 mg daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies weight changes.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Genitourinary: Denies hematuria.

VITALS

Blood pressure is 128/72 mmHg. Heart rate is 79 bpm. Respiratory rate is 26. Oxygen saturation is 99%. No jugular venous distention.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur which is stable.

Genitourinary
- Examination of the Lower Extremities: Trace edema.

RESULTS

Echocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Mr. Johnson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic heart failure.

Acute on chronic heart failure.
• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in an uncontrolled hypertension.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He should avoid pizza and chicken wings. He should also avoid foods that are high in sodium. He should continue to check his blood pressure at home and contact me if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and weight control. He was given information about foods that are low in sodium.

Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: This is well controlled on current medications.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily.
• Medical Reasoning: We will order a renal artery ultrasound to be sure we are not missing another cause of hypertension.

Chronic Kidney Disease.
• Medical Reasoning: We need to make sure he tolerates the regimen.
• Medical Treatment: We will order more labs to check his kidney function.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
