summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right ankle pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Johnson is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain. He states he was going to take out the trash and slipped on some ice. He denies any prior ankle injuries. He is currently unable to bear weight on his right ankle. He reports he is able to walk a little bit today, but he is still limping. The patient states he has been icing and taking ibuprofen, which he says helps. He has been resting his ankle. He is an avid soccer player and has been playing in the community league.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient plays soccer.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports taking ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness.

VITALS

Vitals are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Physical exam shows a pleasant, alert, and oriented male in no distress. Vital signs are normal. Neurological exam of the right foot shows capillary refill less than 3 seconds, strong dorsalis pedis pulse, and intact sensation to light touch distally. Examination of the right ankle shows ecchymosis and bruising over the lateral malleolus associated swelling. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. There is no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right ankle was obtained and reviewed today. It reveals no fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Right ankle sprain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are consistent with right ankle sprain. I have recommended that he continue to elevate his right leg and ice it as needed. I have also recommended that he use an Aircast and provided him with crutches for the next 1 to 2 days. He may start bearing weight as tolerated after that time. I have also recommended that he take NSAIDs as needed for pain. I have advised him that his symptoms should significantly improve in the next 2 weeks. I have recommended that we follow up at that time to determine if he is ready to return to soccer.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will continue to elevate his right leg and ice it as needed. He will use an Aircast and will use crutches for the next 1 to 2 days. He may start bearing weight as tolerated after that time. He will take NSAIDs as needed for pain. He will follow up in 2 weeks to determine if he is ready to return to soccer. }}<|eot_id|>
