Chest pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Elizabeth Rodriguez is a 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for a follow up. She is complaining of chest pain. She has had this for about 3 months now, and it started when she starts walking. It feels like a pressure type of pain. She denies any chest pain right now. She denies any shortness of breath, sweats, or clamminess. She has a past medical history of diabetes and hypertension.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.
Cardiovascular: Denies palpitations.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.
Genitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence.
Musculoskeletal: Denies lower extremity swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck
- General Examination: No JVD.
- Carotid: No bruits.

Respiratory
- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.
- No wheezes or rales.

Cardiovascular
- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.
- No palpable pulses.
- No edema in the lower extremities.

Gastrointestinal
- Examination: No tenderness, guarding, or masses.

Musculoskeletal
- Examination: No tenderness, guarding, or masses.
- Edema in the lower extremities, 1+ nonpitting.

RESULTS

EKG revealed no signs of a heart attack.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Elizabeth Rodriguez is a 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for a follow up. She is complaining of chest pain. She has had this for about 3 months now, and it started when she starts walking. She denies any chest pain right now. She denies any shortness of breath, sweats, or clamminess. She has a past medical history of diabetes and hypertension.

Chest pain.
- Medical Reasoning: Her symptoms are consistent with unstable angina. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. Her family history is also significant for heart disease.
- Medical Treatment: I have referred her to cardiology. I am going to start her on aspirin 81 mg daily and nitroglycerin to take as needed for relief of chest pain. If she takes the nitroglycerin and it does not relieve her symptoms, she should go to the hospital.

Diabetes.
- Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her diet and exercise. Her last hemoglobin A1c was 7. She is scheduled to see ophthalmology in 2 weeks.
- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day. I am going to order a hemoglobin A1c today. Continue to avoid sweets and try to exercise.

Hypertension.
- Medical Reasoning: She has a trend of elevated blood pressures. Several other clinicians have documented this as well.
- Medical Treatment: I am going to start her on hydrochlorothiazide. This will also help with her leg swelling. I would like her to use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her findings into her EPIC MyChart. I am going to keep a log of her blood pressure readings daily in the morning when she wakes up. If we need to make any adjustments to her medications, we can do so at her next visit.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
