summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Davis is a pleasant 32-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books. He denies using proper lifting technique. At first, he did not have any pain, but it has progressively worsened. He describes the pain as "achy and sharp" in his low back. It radiates down his left leg. The pain is not worse with any specific position. He denies any numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness. He denies any loss of control of his bladder or bowels. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol, which sometimes helps. He denies any previous discussions about surgery. The patient is an Olympic weightlifter and has a sedentary job. He tries to exercise a few times a week. He has a history of playing soccer. He denies smoking. He has a father with back pain. The patient reports he is a big fan of the Tatu Hotsper.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.

VITALS

BP: 130/70
RR: 18

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Examination of the spine: Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Positive straight leg raising bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray and MRI of the lumbar spine were reviewed today. The MRI demonstrates disk desiccation, disk bulge, with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT

Lumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we proceed with a lumbar epidural injection with a strong non-steroidal anti-inflammatory (NSAID) and corticosteroid into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. This should help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. We can schedule this today if he would like.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week for a follow-up visit. }}<|eot_id|>
