summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Rodriguez is a pleasant 56-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. She states that she sustained this injury one week ago while playing with her niece. She was running around with her niece who is learning how to ride a bike without training wheels and she fell and twisted her knee. The patient reports that she heard a pop at the time of injury. She states that she is currently limping and rates her pain as a 4 out of 10. She denies the ability to bear weight on the left leg. The patient has been taking Tylenol and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies the use of ibuprofen due to a recent upper respiratory tract infection. The patient states that the Tylenol provides minimal relief.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a recent upper respiratory tract infection.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she was playing with her niece and fell and twisted her knee.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that she is currently taking Tylenol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema present. Effusion present. Moderate range of motion. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative varus and valgus stress tests.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was obtained. It was normal.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain, likely medial collateral ligament strain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her examination findings were not consistent with a torn ligament. I have recommended the following: We will start her on tramadol 50 mg twice a day. I have also recommended that she use Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also recommended that she wear a knee immobilizer for the next week. I also recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her that she should refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. I have also advised her that if she continues to have worsening pain she should contact the office and we can obtain further imaging such as an MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 3 weeks to assess her progress. }}<|eot_id|>
